WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.280
Well, one week ago tonight,

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.360
an unarmed black man named George Floyd

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.600
was killed by a
Minneapolis police officer.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.100
Every day since, people all over the world

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.380
have gathered to demand
change and call attention

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.020
to horrific racial injustices
affecting communities

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.150
of color every day.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.690
Joining me tonight is Van Jones,
CNN's political commentator

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.900
and the CEO of Reform Alliance.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.853
Thank you very much for being with us.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:30.910
I said up front

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.230
that I don't wanna do
much talking tonight.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.380
I'd rather you did the
talking so why don't we start,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.310
what are you feeling
right now, how are you?

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:44.183
You know, I was as sad as I've ever been.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.163
I think from a black
perspective as a parent,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:55.030
we had this one little hope,
this little tiny thread

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.200
that there was something
we could tell our children

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.520
that they could do that
would keep them safe

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.470
from even the worst officers.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.920
If they didn't run, if
they didn't talk back,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.340
if they didn't have drugs on them,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.820
if they didn't drive away,
if they kept their hands

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.200
on the steering wheel, if they
said, yes, sir or no, ma'am

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.950
that, we kinda sprinkled
this little fairy dust

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.880
on our kids every day about
how to survive, you know,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.323
encounters with police.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.470
In this situation, there's
nothing we could've told our kids

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.910
to survive, it was a lynching
from our point of view.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.790
I mean, just literally, in broad daylight,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.860
this guy just, you know,
one minute, two minute,

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.900
three minute, four minute,
five minute, six minute,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.490
seven minutes with people
begging for this guy's life

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.420
and that brings back such
historical trauma for us

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.050
because that used to happen
a lot, just the lynching

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.420
of black men in public with
the community being helpless

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.920
and the lynchings were
designed to humiliate

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.223
and intimidate the whole community.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.640
We can do whatever we want to you

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.490
and you can't do anything about it.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.930
And when it's law enforcement
doing it, you really

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:09.560
feel helpless so that's why I
think you see this incredible

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.833
despair in the black community.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.143
I think that's why you're
seeing this outpouring.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.543
At the same time,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:28.000
through our tears, we, I
think, might start to notice

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.195
that the fear that nobody cares about us,

00:02:32.195 --> 00:02:34.970
that all the white people,
you know, don't understand,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.320
may not be as well founded as we think

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.300
because there's 20, 30,
40, 50 million white people

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.890
who saw that video who were
also shocked and horrified

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.103
and are now saying, hey,

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.723
I might be late to the party
here but what can I do?

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:00.050
Now that is the positive.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.010
You know, out of a breakdown
you can have a breakthrough

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.380
and there is a positive
side of this that nobody's

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.230
defending, I mean, you're not seeing,

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.740
you know, people may argue
about the disturbances

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.880
which we can talk about
but nobody is defending

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.600
what this officer did and it does create

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.330
some small bit of hope that's
starting to rise up in me

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.230
that we could have, at
least, a better conversation.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.520
Look, you're never gonna
get rid of police brutality

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.590
just because, you'll never
get rid of bad schools

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.010
or bad restaurants, I mean,
you're always gonna have human

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.630
beings who just do bad
stuff but you can create

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.770
an environment where when
it happens the response

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.170
is effective enough that,
you know, other people

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.550
don't want to go down that same bad road.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.940
You can close down, you
know, restaurants that are,

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.080
you know, not meeting the
sanitation requirement.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.810
You can fire a teacher,
you can do certain things.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.140
You're never gonna get rid of racism,

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:56.170
you're never gonna get
rid of police brutality

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.940
but you can have a better system

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.000
so that you don't have
lawlessness in law enforcement

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.140
then leading to lawlessness on the street,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.830
that we can do.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:09.443
It feels to me and again, I want,

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.600
you know, I wanna hear

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:13.920
how you're feeling

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:20.550
that there is an anger and
there's also a sense right now,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.993
people are feeling very fragile,

00:04:23.905 --> 00:04:27.170
there's almost a state of people

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.010
really being on the edge and
it's a mixture of feelings.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:34.650
It's not just anger, it's
also, I mean, obviously

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.110
there's a lot of anger
out there and justifiably

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.130
so but it's more than that,
it's a little more complex

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.630
than that because this is not a new story.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.720
This is not the first time it's happened

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.080
and in my lifetime, I mean,
I'm just a couple years old

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.510
when the first big riots hit
after Dr. Martin Luther King

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.370
is shot, there's a feeling
that we've been here again

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.383
and again and again and again.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.800
Does this one feel different
to you for some reason?

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.910
I think it feels different
because there's literally

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:11.210
no excuse, like, all these other ones,

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.120
oh, well, he was running,
well, he was this, well,

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.830
he was talking back or
he punched a person,

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.020
or he was an ax murderer
or whatever it was.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:21.510
I mean, this person, a $20 counterfeit?

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:25.070
You're gonna kill a guy
over a counterfeit $20 bill

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:28.020
that maybe wasn't even his
and it's in broad daylight

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.970
and everything that we have
said, well, let's get body cams.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.440
They had body cams.
Yeah.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:34.330
Well, let's, you know,
make sure community,

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.360
you know, people pay
attention, you know, bring out

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.150
the cell phones, all that
was done and the guy just sat

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.540
there with his hands in his pocket

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:45.450
while he was killing somebody,
so I think that the nature

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:50.130
of it is so much more
shocking, so much more visceral

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:53.320
and there's no excuse, you
can't point to a single thing.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.160
After, listen, even after
minute two, it was justified,

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.770
after minute four,
five, six, seven, eight,

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.120
it wasn't, so--
Right.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.750
So you're now in that world
and so and then I think

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:07.750
the other thing is that the,
when the local prosecutor

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:11.730
came forward and charged
only one of the officers

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:15.500
with third degree murder,
it's like, the most horrific

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.380
crime of the century
recorded for the whole world

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.560
and you get third degree
murder, I've never heard,

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.220
I'm an attorney, I've never
heard of third degree murder.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.730
Yeah, can you do me a
favor and I know that

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:30.020
this is something that you've
studied, you are an attorney.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:34.950
I didn't understand third
degree murder and my 14-year-old

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:37.170
son asked me last night,
"What's third degree murder?"

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.080
And I was embarrassed that I
didn't know and I didn't know

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.410
how that was even applicable,
it didn't make sense to me.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.480
Yeah, third degree murder
is like a oopsy murder.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:51.210
It's like, oops, it'd be
like, if you were driving past

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.990
a house that you thought was
empty and fired your weapon

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:57.240
and oops there was somebody
in there, like, the intention

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.020
was completely innocent but
you were maybe being reckless.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.723
That's what that is, now
here's the problem with it.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:09.000
Prosecutors don't behave
this way with everybody else.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.600
Especially with black
people, with everybody else,

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.760
you'll get 27 charges and
everybody standing around

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.520
will also get arrested and
get at least 15 charges

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.250
so you have maximum number
of people, you know, arrested

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.390
'cause the standard for
arrest is probable cause,

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:26.020
I mean, you can arrest people
for practically nothing.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.590
So everybody gets arrested
and everybody gets charged

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:33.220
with 1,000 things and then
you plead down from there.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:37.870
When this local prosector
gives this cream puff charge

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:41.640
that nobody's ever heard of and
that's it and doesn't arrest

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.470
anyone else, what's he gonna
do, he's gonna plead down

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:48.340
to a traffic ticket, I
mean, you're starting

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.130
with third degree murder?

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.240
That's when the protests,
because everybody in the black

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.200
community, uh uh, this, the fix is in.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.249
And that's when it got
worse not better because

00:07:59.249 --> 00:08:00.550
of the nature of the charge.
Yeah.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:04.340
Because it did feel, there
was also this lag time.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:08.700
Everyone saw that horrible
video, it's completely

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.660
inexplicable and there
was this lag time that

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.390
before anything was
announced, anything was done

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:20.880
and you wonder might
things be different now

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.370
if immediately, people
had come forward and said,

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:28.100
no, this was wrong and here
are the steps we're gonna take,

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.720
an immediate investigation
and these people

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.500
are being placed under arrest.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.590
Look, I think that you'd
have still had protests,

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.280
for sure, 'cause it was just so shocking.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:39.780
You would've had protests but
it would've been a different

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.980
kind and you wouldn't have
had the level of exasperation

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.380
and frustration, I mean, you
still have the three other

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:48.440
officers not charged, the
reason you charge everybody

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:51.470
is because you get people
to turn on each other.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.110
You know and probable cause
is such a low standard,

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.250
you can literally arrest
and charge everybody

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.720
and then you figure it
out, you have leverage

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.600
to figure it out, the idea
they don't even want leverage,

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.610
they don't even want leverage,
I mean, it's a complete

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:08.610
double standard and that's
again, so then it's salt

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.870
in the wound, like, nobody
would be treated this way.

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:14.980
If a white woman were being
treated this way by anybody,

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.590
it would be, I mean, the
entire block would've come out

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:18.730
and just tackled the guy,

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:20.508
I mean--
Yeah.

00:09:20.508 --> 00:09:22.880
And so, and then the
prosecutor's behaving differently

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.563
as well, there is a way out of this.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:30.610
You know, I think a lot of
people just feel total despair.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:33.640
I don't think that that's
where we should stay.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.680
I don't think we should deny the pain

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:39.653
but we shouldn't let the
pain have the last word.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.120
There have been, you know,
there have been lynchings

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.770
in the past, there have
been bad cops in the past

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:49.240
and we've come through
that as a black community,

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.900
as an American community
and there's an opportunity

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:55.150
here, first of all, justice in
this case means all the cops

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:58.440
get charged, there's no way
that, you know, sitting around,

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.010
basically, an accessory to
murder, you should get away

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.210
scot-free, something needs to happen.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:07.210
Number two, there can be now common ground

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:11.080
on this question of
lawlessness in law enforcement.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:14.720
There could be a
bipartisan bill in Congress

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.660
that would do some smart
stuff, like, for instance,

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:19.671
one reason police officers act the way

00:10:19.671 --> 00:10:22.510
that they do, you can't
sue 'em individually.

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.900
So they have literal, total immunity,

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.450
so no matter what happens,
maybe they get demoted or fired,

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.940
they still keep their house,
they still keep their boat,

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.300
they still keep their truck,
so there's a way that that

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.340
needs to be addressed, if
you do something egregious,

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:38.850
you should also be able
to have your personal

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:42.530
fortune at stake, that could be a break.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:46.090
You wanna create checks
and balances and breaks on,

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:47.290
I'm from a law enforcement family.

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:49.160
My father was a cop in the military.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:52.280
My uncle just retired from
Memphis City Police Force.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:56.000
I understand, you know, cops aren't Satan,

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:59.580
they're not super heroes,
they're just city employees.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:02.580
Some are good, some suck,
you gotta have systems

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.640
to deal with the ones that suck
and if you just say because

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:08.670
somebody has on a blue uniform,
I'm gonna back 'em no matter

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:12.270
what, that's not constitutional
democracy, that's gang

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.170
mentality, like, because
of what you're wearing,

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.330
I'm on your side, that's gang mentality.

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:20.840
We're not, the reason,
a democratic republic

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:24.320
trusts a small group of
armed men and now women

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:27.850
with a monopoly on violence, why?

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:30.410
Because there's checks, there's balances,

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.880
there's process, there's
laws, so we entrust you

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:37.040
with a monopoly on violence in a system.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:41.270
Once you step out of that system
and you start abusing that,

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:44.440
if the system doesn't pull
you back, now you've just got

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:46.970
lawlessness of the worst kind.

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:50.640
You have, essentially, a number
of gangs in the community,

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.990
one of them just happens
to have on a badge.

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:55.640
That's not what you want
but that's what you've got

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:59.330
in way too many neighborhoods
and way too many cities

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:03.260
and right and left should
disagree with that reality.

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:05.960
Right and left should come
together and say, let's fix that.

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:08.560
Let's put some more tools in
place, put some more checks

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:11.460
and balances in place so
that you can have the police

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:14.780
obeying the law and therefore
the citizenry obeying the law

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:17.130
and that's how you have
peaceful streets and nobody

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:20.550
wants peaceful streets more,
nobody wants peaceful streets

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:23.330
more than African Americans,
we're not a part of some

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:27.240
pro riot lobby or some pro crime lobby.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.700
I'm raising two black
boys here in Los Angeles,

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.240
I do not want riots here,
I just want lawful policing

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.140
and peaceful streets
just like everybody else.

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:38.810
Yeah, there's, I don't know how you react

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.810
to, this seems to happen whenever

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.970
there's an incident like this

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:47.480
and sadly, there are way too many of them,

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:49.620
you have people in the
white community saying

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.350
and I'm gonna say the white
liberal community saying,

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:56.270
I didn't realize, I didn't
quite realize the extent

00:12:56.270 --> 00:13:00.830
and it feels a little bit
like we've expired on that,

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:04.730
you know, that the time
for people to be saying,

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:07.480
I didn't realize, well,
I didn't quite understand

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:12.060
how bad it was, I'm catching
up now, that we have past

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.970
that point quite a while ago

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:17.600
and that it's, there is no excuse.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.213
Yeah, hey, listen,

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:22.660
I feel that way now, I do.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:23.923
There's a fatigue,

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:26.593
this is just a fatigue.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:33.580
You know, why do you pay
money to go to college?

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.670
Why do you pay tuition?

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:40.570
Because educating
somebody is costly, okay?

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:41.723
It's costly.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:45.820
If I have to sit here and
educate every one of my white

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:48.989
friends every time something happens,

00:13:48.989 --> 00:13:51.660
I have to educate one of my
white friends every Facebook

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:55.330
post from a high school
buddy, every well intentioned

00:13:55.330 --> 00:14:00.330
colleague, it is costly and
they not paying me no tuition.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:04.631
It's just a pure cost.
Right.

00:14:04.631 --> 00:14:06.840
And at a certain point, you're
like, can you do your own

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:11.840
homework, can you just watch
the "13th" documentary?

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.880
Can you just read "The New Jim Crow?"

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.410
Just do a little bit so I don't
have to do all of it myself

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.480
every day and usually I'm
surrounded and outnumbered.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.210
If you're a professional
black person, upper class

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:24.730
black person, frankly we're
only 12% of the country,

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.790
most of the time, you're
surrounded and outnumbered

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:29.420
and you're gonna have to
now try one more time,

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:32.220
create a free college for somebody else,

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:33.990
that, you know, you don't benefit from,

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:36.370
you actually are drained
by it, it's tough.

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:40.130
And you know, James
Baldwin said it the best.

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:42.710
He said, "When it comes to issues of race,

00:14:42.710 --> 00:14:44.700
white people are always innocent."

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:47.330
They're always innocent
and their innocence

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.023
constitutes their crime.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:52.740
How can you be innocent this
late, you know what I mean?

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:56.100
Quit being innocent, this
innocence act is now criminal.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:58.300
Yeah.
Just admit

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:00.700
we live in a country
with a terrible history,

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:03.660
nobody likes that reality,
it's uncomfortable

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:07.860
but the fact that you can
run away from it, 364 days

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.400
out of the year doesn't mean you should.

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:13.800
It's just a part of being in a democratic,

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.530
multi-racial, multi-cultural,
multi-faith democracy,

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:19.550
we have to work to learn about each other

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:22.280
and if you're in the
majority, the whole country

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.330
conspires to make sure
you know your own history,

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.157
they call that American
history, you have to do a little

00:15:26.157 --> 00:15:28.490
bit more work to learn the Latino history,

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:30.320
a little bit more work to
learn the black history,

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:32.050
a little bit more work to
learn the Eskimo history,

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:35.100
whatever you wanna call
it but that's the price

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:38.810
of being a good citizen in
a multi-racial democracy

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:41.070
and we're one of the few in
the history of the world.

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:43.790
It takes work and all that
work shouldn't just fall

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:45.933
on my shoulders to do the educating.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.670
I think there was and I
think you've recently quoted

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:56.780
it but it was an interesting
quote, I think from Malcolm X,

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:00.950
that he was and you'll
correct me, you probably

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:04.260
know it better than I do
but I think he said he was

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:07.110
sometimes more afraid
of a liberal, you know,

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:11.120
someone who was well
intentioned but ignorant

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.680
than he was of an outright bigot

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:17.750
and felt that in a way that
could be more insidious.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:19.580
Do you feel that that's something

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:21.580
that we might be experiencing right now?

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:24.520
Yeah, you know, I got
into a lot of trouble

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.030
earlier in the week, pointing
it out but, you know,

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:29.100
we're all friends here, at
some point we have to speak

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:33.240
honestly with each
other and there is a way

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:36.280
and Malcolm, you know, he
talked about the southern wolf

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:39.440
versus the northern fox, he
said, that southern wolf,

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.240
you know, he bares his
teeth at ya, and you know

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.310
he's mad at ya, you know
he doesn't want you around.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:46.220
He said that northern fox, you know,

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:49.230
he might be smiling, looks
a little bit more friendly

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:52.320
but it's still, you know,
you're still in real danger

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:55.230
and so I do think we have
to look at the fact that

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:58.930
a lot of people who vote for
democrats, who support causes,

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:02.140
who share all the right
stuff on their Instagram page

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:05.670
and all that sort of stuff,
you know, to be honest,

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:08.090
don't have a lot of
personal black friends,

00:17:08.090 --> 00:17:10.790
don't have a lot of
personal skin in the game,

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:13.070
don't hire a lot of African Americans,

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:17.710
don't have African American
lawyers or accountants

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:19.870
or dentists or you know, places
where the black community

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:22.630
does have a lot of
professionals stacked up,

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:25.220
they don't avail themselves
of those services.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:27.970
They will call the cops
just like that woman

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.540
in Central Park, you know, in an instant

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.220
and you know, and act just like, I mean,

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.300
the Ku Klux Klan wouldn't
have acted any better

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:37.340
or any worse than that
white woman in Central Park

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:41.120
calling the police on that
black man for no reason and--

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:42.790
He was a bird watcher.

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:44.600
He was a bird watcher.
Yeah.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:46.200
I mean, I'm really scared
of a bird watcher, you know,

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:50.890
in the park, so these
are the kinds of things,

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:53.790
there's a real opportunity
because if you're gonna be honest

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:57.883
and this is the worst
thing I'll say tonight.

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.620
If you're gonna be honest,

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:02.730
there is a way in which

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:07.040
white people choking off opportunity,

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:11.110
choking off dignity,
choking off the ability

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:14.590
to thrive and rise and
looking like they're not

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.090
doing anything wrong and
blaming the black people,

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:21.310
that's kind of America's
thing, it's a little bit

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:25.230
of our thing and when you
look in the face of that guy,

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:27.040
he looked like he wasn't
doing anything wrong,

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:28.250
he had his hands in his
pockets and he's literally

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:30.810
killing somebody and if
you ask him why, he'll say,

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:33.850
because this guy's a threat,
well, hold on a second.

00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:35.990
You have to have a very
particular world view to look

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:38.390
at this picture and think the
guy down there is a threat

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:40.290
and that you're the innocent hero

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.290
and yet, we kinda do that, don't we?

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.360
Board room after board room,
hiring decision after hiring

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:48.800
decision, bank decision
after bank decision,

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:52.250
somehow, always the same
group gets choked out

00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:54.690
and the other group benefits
and they're innocent

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:57.530
and so these are the kinds of
things that I would encourage

00:18:57.530 --> 00:18:59.640
people to think about, is there some way

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:01.290
that I'm like that guy?

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.050
You know, am I kinda like that cop?

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:05.193
Would I overreact, am I afraid?

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:08.854
Am I giving enough oxygen?

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.100
To the black people in my community.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:15.195
Do I have enough of the--

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:20.630
Awareness of the pain.

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.380
Do I have enough awareness
of the suffering?

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:26.710
Or am I looking away when
everybody else in the world

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:29.560
can see, man, that community's
suffering in your country.

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:33.250
Yeah, I think what you're pointing out

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:35.820
and which makes a lot of sense to me,

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:38.220
is it's very easy for people
to watch that footage,

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:43.220
that horrible footage,
demonize those officers,

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:47.820
say that's terrible, I feel
so terrible for you people

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:53.930
and I am on your side and it
can all feel like an empty

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:59.840
exercise, it can all feel
hollow and meaningless

00:20:00.520 --> 00:20:03.430
as opposed to how did
we get to that point?

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:04.610
Right.
And what can we

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:07.520
do going forward, I'm curious,

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:10.807
I try to avoid the Trump of things

00:20:10.807 --> 00:20:14.230
not for any other reason
that it can be such a big

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:18.010
distraction and I consider
it a victory when I have long

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:20.370
conversations with people
where he is not discussed

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:23.360
or he does not monopolize
the conversation.

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:25.390
Me too.
What could

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:31.760
a president do now today
that would be meaningful?

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:35.900
Let's forget for a moment that,

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:38.730
which president it is, what
could a president do today

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:42.640
that would be meaningful and be a salve

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:45.640
to the African American community,

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:48.770
make them feel better
and make them feel like

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:51.113
they were heard, what would it take?

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.910
Well, you know, ironically, some of things

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:58.500
that Trump is actually doing, ironically.

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:00.910
You know, have the Department of Justice

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:03.040
and the FBI get in there and figure out

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:04.140
what the heck is going on.

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:06.713
That has been done and is being done.

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:11.160
You know, receive a briefing
directly in the oval office

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:14.513
about it and then speak out
publicly which he also did.

00:21:15.970 --> 00:21:18.760
The situation that we're in,
is it's the things that he

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:21.790
should stop doing that cause the problems.

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:23.540
The tweeting and this sort of stuff

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:28.950
but there, you know, I'm trying
to be fair with everybody,

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:31.500
there have been things that
the Trump administration

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.513
has done that are appropriate.

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.450
He should stop doing certain
things and then beyond

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:38.863
what has been done.

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:44.023
You know, we need to get together.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:46.610
We need to come together.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:49.430
If we don't come together,
we're gonna end up

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:52.573
where we're headed and where
we're headed is not good.

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:56.680
You could have, we're about
one more of these video tapes

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:58.880
away from having, you know,
five or six American cities

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:02.130
burned to the ground, I mean,
this is not a good trajectory

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:06.350
to be on as a country and
nobody would like that outcome.

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:09.120
So we have an outcome that's
looming that nobody wants,

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:10.670
you can get everybody together.

00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:13.980
Man, would it be great for everybody

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.010
to go to the white house.

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:19.247
Everybody, NAACP, the NRA,

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:21.960
I mean, everybody just
go to the white house

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:23.950
and just sit down and just cry

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:26.710
and just have some ministers
come and pray over everybody

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:31.710
and for secular people, you
know, have some musicians come

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:34.280
and just have a big cry

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:36.253
and then

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:40.690
listen to law enforcement say
how they don't like what's

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:43.300
going on and they're
willing to make some changes

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:47.090
because obviously, something's
going on that they don't want

00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:50.160
and listen to some of those
young kids talk about how,

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:51.760
I mean, those kids are so heartbreaking.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:53.610
I just wanna be able to
go to a white neighborhood

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:56.960
and not feel afraid, I mean simple things

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:00.903
and just get together,
that's what I would like.

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:05.470
Just get together, this is, you know,

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:08.450
when the funeral happens, I
mean, wouldn't it be amazing

00:23:08.450 --> 00:23:12.540
if all the republican leaders
and all the democrat leaders

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:14.790
were sittin' in there, I
mean, I guess we gotta social

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:16.370
distance or whatever but, you know,

00:23:16.370 --> 00:23:20.100
whatever it is, this is
a time to say, listen,

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:22.420
I can score points and
you can score points.

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:24.580
I can call you a racist and
you can call me a socialist.

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:27.537
I can say you you're Antifa,
you can say I'm a Nazi.

00:23:27.537 --> 00:23:31.250
I mean we can do this, we've
been doing this now for years

00:23:31.250 --> 00:23:35.040
but this one, you gotta
draw a circle around

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:37.960
and Americans are drawing
a circle around it.

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:40.520
I mean, the disturbances, that's different

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:45.150
but this particular murder,
Americans have drawn a circle

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:50.150
around, there is room now,
I think, for a president

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:51.610
to bring everybody together.

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:55.460
I wanna make sure I
mention, Reform Alliance,

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:58.990
this is something, you're
the CEO of Reform Alliance

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:02.720
and talk about taking action
to move the puzzle piece

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:05.313
a little forward, tell us what it is.

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:11.300
Well, you know, Jay-Z and
Meek Mill and Robert Smith

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:14.130
and Robert Kraft and a
bunch of billionaires

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:17.800
including Clara Wu Tsai
and Novogratz and Dan Loeb

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:22.220
and Laura Arnold, all got together

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:25.950
a year and a half ago and said
too many are going to jail

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:27.930
for petty stuff and too
many people are caught up

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:30.710
on probation and parole for
too long and they're going back

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:33.790
to jail for petty stuff, let's
do something to try to have

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:36.090
fewer people going to jail for petty stuff

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:40.990
and 'cause you shouldn't
deprive people of their liberty

00:24:40.990 --> 00:24:45.840
lightly and then between the COVID thing

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:48.100
which has now made the
jails full of this plague

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:50.750
and people are dying in
the prisons in huge numbers

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:55.030
and then this incident,
we realize, we are also,

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:57.120
we're not just trying to protect liberty,

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:59.450
we're trying to protect people's lives

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:01.810
and we partnered with
Jack Dorsey from Twitter

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:04.630
to get masks into all
the jails and prisons.

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:07.420
We have legislation moving
in a number of states

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:10.430
to try to reduce the number
of people going to jail

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:12.780
for probation and parole
violations, little non-crime

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:15.860
technical violations, a
lot of things like that

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:20.360
but this thing set us on our butt

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:23.320
and Jay-Z himself picked
up the phone and called

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:27.290
the governor of Minnesota
and asked him personally

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:30.810
to make sure that the case
was given to the state

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:33.290
attorney general, who happens
to be African American

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:36.060
but is also just a fair minded prosecutor

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:38.370
so that it wouldn't, we
wouldn't keep going down

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:40.990
this road of having the local
prosecutor screw up the case

00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:43.150
and then people burn down the country

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:45.870
and thankfully for that
reason and many others,

00:25:45.870 --> 00:25:48.800
the governor did give the
case to a better prosecutor.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.190
So when you have a
better chance of justice,

00:25:51.190 --> 00:25:56.110
I think you have a better
chance of a bipartisan bill

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:59.180
that could really fix law
enforcement in some important ways

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:02.261
and there also needs to
be a better chance to try

00:26:02.261 --> 00:26:04.350
to put some money into these communities.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:06.390
All these corporations and
banks should get together,

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:09.480
let's help these communities,
we see what's going on.

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:13.350
If you have justice, legislation,
and economic opportunity,

00:26:13.350 --> 00:26:15.781
we can come out of this
stronger and better

00:26:15.781 --> 00:26:18.130
and that's, I'm starting to
feel some hope now for that,

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:19.890
I'm starting to feel some hope.

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:22.390
You know, we didn't really
even get much of a chance

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:27.390
to talk before we hooked up on Zoom today

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:29.910
and so I wasn't sure
what your state of mind

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:33.700
was gonna be and again, I
wanted you to do the talking

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:38.700
and I'm hearing pain and I'm
hearing anguish and frustration

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:42.130
but I'm also hearing some
hope that there might

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:45.430
be an opportunity here
and I wanna be careful

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:47.840
when I say that and I'm
saying there's an opportunity

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.980
in a horrible situation but
there might be an opportunity

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:55.980
here to move ahead, to move
up and to be a little better.

00:26:58.467 --> 00:27:03.467
I think so, I think if
people don't stop at pity

00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:07.280
because pity burns out,
it doesn't feel good.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:11.150
If we move to opportunity, if
we move to say, hey, listen,

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:13.190
who knows, I mean, that
guy that got killed,

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:15.140
he might have cured
cancer for all we know,

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:17.340
we don't who the heck that
guy was or what his kids

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:18.910
could've become or whatever.

00:27:18.910 --> 00:27:20.370
Right, right.
So we're wasting

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:22.970
some genius, we're missing out
on some possible best friends

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:26.630
we ever had and some possible
great employees or employers

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:28.973
or contractors or neighbors.

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:31.850
What can I do in my personal life?

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:33.750
People go, how can I fix
the police department?

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:37.240
Trust me, that's not easy
but your own workplace,

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:39.490
you might be able to get an
internship program in place,

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:42.370
you might be able to mentor somebody.

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:45.510
Don't forget how much you
know and not from a book.

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:47.150
How much you know
because somebody sat down

00:27:47.150 --> 00:27:48.410
and talked to you.

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:50.130
One of the problems when
you're black is it's hard

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:52.320
to find a person who'll
talk to you, honestly.

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:54.180
You know, the conversation
gets stiff or tense

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:56.898
or whatever or it just
doesn't happen because

00:27:56.898 --> 00:27:59.610
you don't even know that that
building you're driving past

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:03.380
is a lodge or a country
club, it's just a building,

00:28:03.380 --> 00:28:07.110
you've never been invited in
there and so are there people

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.410
you can mentor, the people
who, and the thing about

00:28:09.410 --> 00:28:11.380
mentorship is you always
get more out of it then you

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:14.860
give anyway, it's not charity,
you wind up so much smarter

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:17.970
and so much more interesting,
you know, oh, I'm talkin'

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:20.340
to my kid from the other side of town

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:22.630
and now you're the most
interesting person at Thanksgiving.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:25.550
You know, so you always
get more than you give

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:27.550
but those are the things that
I would ask people to do.

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:30.830
We have to change some
laws but I tell you what,

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:34.640
if we start changing
who's in our phone book,

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:37.410
who's in our cell phone,
who are we texting with

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:40.970
every couple of days, who
we, that's really where

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:43.750
the change has got to start
happening because then,

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:46.820
you know, suddenly, you
know, somebody says something

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:49.010
in a conversation, and
say, hey, hold on a second,

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:52.540
that's not funny, I know a guy who's black

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:55.540
and I've learned the hard
way, that can wind up

00:28:55.540 --> 00:28:58.750
killing somebody and people
are, what, we didn't know

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:02.060
that you were feeling that way,
well, I do feel that way now

00:29:02.060 --> 00:29:04.560
and suddenly you start
wiping this stuff out

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:07.270
at the social level long before
it ever gets to the legal

00:29:07.270 --> 00:29:09.023
level and I think that's important.

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:12.740
Well, I cannot thank you enough

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:16.573
for doing this today.

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:20.240
I've always loved talking
to you in the past,

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:21.900
we've had really good conversations

00:29:21.900 --> 00:29:26.491
and I did not wanna hear my voice today.

00:29:26.491 --> 00:29:30.000
I was really happy that it
could be your voice, so--

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.360
Hey, listen, you've
always been good to me,

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:34.270
you know, every time I've
had a show launch or a book,

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:36.920
you've always had me on and
you know, I'm glad it created

00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:39.440
a friendship between us and
now we can use it to try

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:40.750
to bring a little bit of hope to people

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:42.610
and I'll come back anytime
you'll have me, sir.

00:29:42.610 --> 00:29:45.730
Thank you so much, this
was very meaningful to me

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:50.730
and I, stay well and good luck
with your kids and handling

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:56.210
all of this and let's keep
the conversation going.

00:29:56.210 --> 00:29:57.780
Will do, thank you very much.

00:29:57.780 --> 00:29:58.733
Thank you, sir.

